      "text": "To determine the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly, we need to consider the area and the dimensions of both the square and the rectangles.\n\nFirst, calculate the area of the square. Let the side length of the square be \\( n \\). The area of the square is \\( n^2 \\).\n\nNext, calculate the area of one 2-by-3 rectangle. The area of one rectangle is \\( 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\).\n\nTo cover the square exactly with non-overlapping 2-by-3 rectangles, the area of the square must be equal to the total area covered by the rectangles. Therefore, we need:\n\\[ n^2 = 6m \\]\nwhere \\( m \\) is the number of rectangles needed. This means \\( n^2 \\) must be a multiple of 6. The smallest integer \\( n \\) that satisfies this condition is 3, because:\n\\[ 3^2 = 9 \\]\nand 9 is a multiple of 6.\n\nNow, let's verify that 3-by-3 and 2-by-3 rectangles can indeed cover a 3-by-3 square. We need to find a valid arrangement.\n\nConsider the following arrangement of 2-by-3 rectangles within a 3-by-3 square:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{R} & \\text{R} & \\text{R} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{R} & \\text{R} & \\text{R} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{R} & \\text{R} & \\text{R} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nHere, \"R\" represents a rectangle. We can see that this arrangement uses:\n\\[ 3 \\text{ rectangles} \\]\neach of which is 2-by-3. Therefore, the total number of rectangles needed is:\n\\[ 3 \\]\n\nThus, the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a 3-by-3 square exactly is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
