      "text": "To determine the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly, we first need to understand the area of both the square and the rectangles.\n\nThe area of a 2-by-3 rectangle is:\n\\[ 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\text{ square units} \\]\n\nIf we denote the side length of the square by \\( n \\), then the area of the square is:\n\\[ n^2 \\text{ square units} \\]\n\nTo cover the square exactly, the area of the square must be a multiple of the area of one of the rectangles. Therefore, \\( n^2 \\) must be a multiple of 6. The smallest \\( n \\) that satisfies this condition is the least common multiple (LCM) of 2 and 3, which is 6. For \\( n = 6 \\), the area of the square is:\n\\[ 6^2 = 36 \\text{ square units} \\]\n\nNow, we need to check if \\( 36 \\) can be covered with whole 2-by-3 rectangles. The number of 2-by-3 rectangles needed is:\n\\[ \\frac{36}{6} = 6 \\]\n\nThus, the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6} \\]",
