      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the smallest number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly without gaps or overhangs, we start by calculating the area of the square and the area of one rectangle.\n\nFirst, let's denote the side length of the square by \\( n \\). The area of the square is \\( n^2 \\). The area of one 2-by-3 rectangle is \\( 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\). To cover the square exactly, the area of the square must be equal to the area of the rectangle multiplied by the number of rectangles needed. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[ n^2 = 6k \\]\n\nwhere \\( k \\) is the number of rectangles needed. Since \\( k \\) must be an integer, \\( n^2 \\) must be a multiple of 6. The smallest integer \\( n \\) that satisfies this condition is \\( n = 6 \\) (since \\( 6^2 = 36 \\) and 36 is the smallest number that is a multiple of 6). Now, we check if 36 can indeed be covered by 6 identical 2-by-3 rectangles:\n\n\\[ 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\]\n\nIndeed, 36 can be divided into 6 rectangles of 2-by-3. Thus, the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{6} \\]",
